[FINAL-TEXT] **Found it.** The smoking gun is on **mswitch1**:

```
eth0.10@eth0: <BROADCAST,MULTICAST> mtu 1500 master br_vlan10 state DOWN
```

`mswitch1.eth0.10` is **DOWN** (bridge state: `disabled`), while the VLAN-20 counterpart `eth0.20@eth0` is **UP**. This is the exact interface in the VLAN-10 forwarding path between switch1 and router0. All other `br_vlan10` members on mswitch1 (`eth2.10`, `eth4.10`) are forwarding normally — the fault is isolated to this one sub-interface.

The team was looking on the wrong device (router0 instead of mswitch1), and at the correct VLAN (10) but the wrong hop.

=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: mswitch1
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** The VLAN-10 sub-interface `eth0.10` on `mswitch1` — the port that connects mswitch1 to switch1 (physical link `link_msw1_sw1`, collision domain `link_msw1_sw1`) — is administratively/operationally DOWN. Its bridge state is `disabled` within `br_vlan10`.

**Mechanism:** switch1 implements VLAN separation by hosting two Linux bridges: `br_vlan10` (serving pc4, pc5, and access ports eth1–eth6) and `br_vlan20` (serving laptop0 via eth7). Traffic from pc4/pc5 egresses switch1's uplink as VLAN-10-tagged 802.1Q frames on `switch1.eth0.10 → eth0`, arriving at mswitch1's `eth0`. mswitch1 is supposed to receive those tagged frames on its own `eth0.10` sub-interface, which is a member of mswitch1's `br_vlan10`, and relay them onward to `eth4.10` → router0.eth0 → router0.eth0.10 (172.16.10.1). Because `mswitch1.eth0.10` is DOWN, it cannot accept any VLAN-10 frames from switch1, so ARP requests from pc4/pc5 for the gateway address never reach router0. The kernel on each PC generates its own "Destination Host Unreachable" ICMP because no ARP reply ever arrives.

**Localization:** `mswitch1`, interface `eth0.10` (the VLAN-10 sub-interface of its `eth0` uplink toward switch1).

**Root cause:** `mswitch1.eth0.10` is in link-down state, severing the only L2 forwarding path for VLAN 10 between switch1 and the rest of the network, while VLAN 20 (using `mswitch1.eth0.20`, which is UP) continues to work — explaining why laptop0 is unaffected.

**Fix:** On mswitch1, execute `ip link set eth0.10 up` to restore the VLAN-10 sub-interface. router0's `eth0.10` (172.16.10.1/24) is correctly configured and UP — no changes needed there.
=== END ===